<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Macedonia (Roman province)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Roman Macedonia, Macedonia Prima</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Macedonia was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and Empire, created in 146 BCE after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had defeated the last Macedonian kings and suppressed the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriscus">Andriscus</a>. Governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, it covered most of the old kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, Paeonia and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a> crossed it from the Adriatic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. In late antiquity it was divided into smaller provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macedonia-roman-province.jpg" alt="Macedonia (Roman province)" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Macedonia_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
